Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
i wanted to share something new i've  been working on uh it started around  february 2021 where i was struck with  this idea of blending both an online  course a public exhibition an internship  or field trip all into one experience as  a part of the problem i always like to  think of a big linear narrative people  travel through  and this was spurred by  frustration with what i saw being done  in the world of online learning  especially during the lockdowns and what  annoyed me most was seeing my own kids  being forced to use technology backwards  not as a tool but as a hurdle  but the big realization for me is that  most industries now operate fully remote  so taking advantage of that and peeking  behind the scenes of any industry in the  world would be easier than ever  and i think the seed i kept coming back  to was the anti-master class which is  kind of a bait and switch in my opinion  where they sell access to your heroes  and what you get is paywall videos  i think about two years ago i was  browsing user reviews of the platform  and  and noticed someone said like the best  part of masterclass was when they got a  comment from someone else in the section  in the comment section on their work  and this felt like kind of a golden  nugget which is that rich user to user  interaction which powers most online  products was missing mainly from online  education or just bolted on  almost everyone i've worked with always  thought of the online community as  secondary to something else the lecture  the multiple choice question the  interactive simulator and the proof was  in the pudding even today no online  course the traditional ones can retain  their users why didn't kids want to  watch videos and do multiple choice  tests even when we had bells and  whistles added on but ultimately it was  mainly about telling so our approach  would be to shift the focus on doing so  our idea is simple what if we designed a  product entirely around making your work  better  every decision on the product would  would answer that question if it doesn't  make your work better we don't need it  and to focus this point slightly what if  we made a program that helped you make  industry-grade prototypes because in a  world where your portfolio is much more  important than your resume this does  seem like the obvious direction  here is the first napkin sketch of this  idea which i kept and the first was  taking the best of school and work and  finding that intersection the second  idea was using the industry prototype  whether it is a game prototype a script  a business pitch  a story reel as the goal post so that  our user builds a mental model for how  prototypes are designed and made in  various industries including the roles  involved i think of this as like if the  user had a parent who worked in that  industry  then they would naturally absorb that  mental model from your parent and so  imagine you had 50 parents in every  industry at all times  what that could do for your future the  third is that the real world work it  happens in teams it requires feedback  and iteration and it's eventually  consumed at a deadline which is where  this idea of exhibition is part of the  process  and so what this product would do is  push people ahead towards being excited  about making x and knowing their role in  that process of creation  and giving all young people access to  this kind of guidance and mentorship as  early as possible would brighten the  future for all  in the past few months we rapidly built  our first prototype of this idea and  designed a new platform from scratch to  accomplish it and so story experiential  allows you to follow the pixar process  for nine weeks to create a prototype of  your own original short film and this  program has the following cadence where  each week you work on a deliverable that  leads you towards the final goal of a  finished prototype  and the pattern is on monday there is a  live session with a different pixar  industry host or two who speak to your  work ahead take live questions and do  some feedback and modeling and then  during the week users work  asynchronously inside their teams or  across teams using our community tools  to get to their weekly deliverable which  they upload on friday or into the  weekend and the really fun part is on  friday people start uploading their work  in progress to our system which leads to  the real engine of our program the  weekly gallery it's a fire hose of work  which everyone in the program is sharing  and critically every user in the program  reviews several other pieces of work so  nobody is ignored and we do that with a  randomizing system in a sorting system  we're still experimenting with the best  way to do this but it is working so far  so every user wakes up monday morning  with their own dedicated page with their  work in progress at the top  and a stream of feedback at the bottom  and so instead of handing in your work  to your teacher to get a check mark it  goes out to peers you don't know  and care about your work which i think  is really interesting  and one of the big surprises is the  gallery acts as the teacher it's way  better than what one person can do  because you're absorbing the work of  hundreds or thousands of people both  conceptually and technically getting  ideas and it ends with the final  exhibition which is the capstone to each  kind of session where all the users get  a dedicated page and a public gallery  kind of netflix style and we do user  choice awards celebration videos  and industry reaction videos to the top  work and now we're still brainstorming  the potential of what this exhibition  can be because this is basically a fire  hose of talent which is being  self-selected and coming out and so  we're imagining in the future being able  to connect people to internships jobs  scholarship opportunities and the  potential becomes enormous when you  imagine doing this experiential model in  game design in music and all these other  areas and finally scale this is where  things get exciting most mentorship  programs make the mistake of physical  mentorship where they think oh we'll get  one expert and 10 kids one expert and 50  kids but our model is designed to allow  one mentor to end people at very low  cost and so far this has required some  clever design and technology  but i'm convinced it can scale based on  our first pilot which we did with about  500 students of all the students who  started the program over 90  completed  which was beyond even our most rosy  projections and it speaks to the fact  that the mechanisms in this program are  working our next step is to scale it up  to many many thousand and see what we  learn and now we're about to launch our  second session new and improved for both  under 18 and over 18 which will keep us  separate cohorts and what i would just  love is more feedback about how to make  this program better and the way to do  that is to have people do the program  and tell us how to make it better so i  invite you to check it out um and if not  you maybe you know someone that would  this is up the rally for their kids or  adults you know who may want to try it  and would be happy to basically just  stream feedback here below this video  and i'll respond to everything so thank  you so much  think of your favorite pixar film  it all starts with a great story  and every great story starts with a team  of storytellers  [Music]  we all tell stories  it's part of everyday life  but how do you turn storytelling into a  career  story experiential with pixar lets you  find out firsthand  through this eight-week program you  won't just see what it's like to work at  pixar you'll do it yourself you can make  a really simple storyboard with very  little animation and tell a great story  but you can also make a beautifully  animated short that has a terrible story  and we think it's more important to have  a good story  here you'll create a story reel for your  own original concept following the same  process that pixar artists use to  develop their movies when you're doing  storyboarding to develop a shorthand for  your character i think it's really  helpful to think about reaction better  than oxygen this is why we edit you are  arming yourself with some story clippers  and you're trimming cutting shaping and  refining your reels into the shape that  you want them to be with weekly guidance  and support from the people at pixar  you'll discover what it takes to tell a  great story  you're creating a memory of like what  happened but so often when you tell a  story  you're creating a feeling  but no one's looking at the degree they  don't need to see your degree to be an  artist right  what they want to see that they want to  feel  is the passion the love that you have  for what you do working in groups you'll  learn the essential skills of giving and  receiving feedback not to mention the  importance of iteration  at the end you'll walk away with a  finished story reel for your portfolio  and the opportunity to have your work  seen at a final exhibition  you'll discover that there's more to  storytelling than writing there's more  to art than drawing  because ultimately the best way to know  whether a job is right for you  is to experience it for yourself

